<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhadarwah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bhaderwah, Bhadrawah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhadarwah is a town in an upland valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doda-district">Doda district</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ruled by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs, it was for long periods a dependency of Chamba State and later came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rulers of Jammu and Kashmir. Its people speak Bhadarwahi, and its temples of the serpent god Vasuki Nag are important local shrines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhadarwah.jpg" alt="Bhadarwah" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manjeet singh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhaderwahi_folk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
